 ## Storage
 
-Sessions live in Postgres ([keep sessions in the database](../adr/0001-keep-sessions-in-the-database.md)).
+Sessions live in signed cookies; the Postgres `sessions` table has been dropped. `sign(member_id, last_request)` in
+[`larder/sessions.py`](../../larder/sessions.py) encodes JSON containing `member` and `expires` as URL-safe base64,
+then appends a dot and an HMAC-SHA256 signature. The expiry is an ISO-formatted timestamp calculated from the last
+request. The signature protects integrity; the payload is readable, not encrypted.
+
+`verify(cookie, now)` checks the signature and expiry and returns the member id, or `None` for a signature mismatch
+or an expiry earlier than `now` (the default is the current UTC time). Malformed cookies can raise decoding or
+parsing errors; this function does not catch them. The repository contains these helpers, but no HTTP integration
+showing cookie attributes or issuance and renewal on requests.
+
+## Session key and logout
+
+Every web server must use the same `LARDER_SESSION_KEY`, supplied through deploy secrets. The module reads it at
+import time; without it, it falls back to `dev-only`, a development value that must not be used in production.
+A deploy preserves sessions when the key stays the same.
+
+To rotate the key, replace the deploy secret and reload or restart every web process so each imports the new value.
+Cookies signed with the previous key then fail verification, logging everyone out. There is no previous-key fallback;
+servers with different keys will disagree about valid cookies during a rollout. Schedule rotation between pickups,
+as agreed in [issue 63](../../notes/issue-63.md). This repository does not specify deployment commands.
+
+There is no server-side session record to revoke for one member. An issued cookie remains usable until its expiry
+unless the shared key changes; removing a cookie from a browser cannot invalidate a retained copy.
 
